WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.640
Americans generally think of Brazil&nbsp;
as a country of beaches, jungles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.120
and Christ the Redeemer: a friendly tourist&nbsp;
destination with fun and festivities. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.000
Brazil isn’t just a nice spot for a vacation.&nbsp;
It is one of the most unequal countries in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.720
world. But it’s also a country with one of the&nbsp;
most successful socialist movements of our time.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.760
In 1888, Brazil abolished slavery – the&nbsp;
last country in the Americas to do so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.600
But even after a republic was established in&nbsp;
1889, elites excluded most people from voting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.360
using their power to enrich&nbsp;
themselves and their families.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.640
For decades, Brazil was a textbook&nbsp;
oligarchy, based on “coffee with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.160
milk politics” – a reference to the agrarian&nbsp;
economies of the two most influential states.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.680
The government acted above all to protect elite&nbsp;
interests while millions languished in poverty.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.960
As Brazil urbanized, peasants flocking to&nbsp;
cities could see prosperity around them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.080
but couldn’t reach it. They built sprawling&nbsp;
informal settlements called “favelas,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.320
Stark illustrations of the country’s disparity.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.480
By the early 1960s, an alternative vision&nbsp;
emerged, based on land redistribution and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:19.120
expanded democracy. Communists, peasants, workers,&nbsp;
and students supported this reformist push.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:25.440
But then, fearing a communist takeover, the United&nbsp;
States and local elites supported a military coup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.160
From 1964 to 1985, Brazil was&nbsp;
ruled by a BRUTAL dictatorship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.400
that killed and tortured thousands of people.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.120
But by the late 1970s, something was changing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.520
Workers noticed their wages were not&nbsp;
keeping pace with the rising cost of living.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.520
It turned out they were getting paid&nbsp;
significantly less than the regime let on.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.760
So, led by a combative, charismatic union leader&nbsp;
named Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva – commonly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.840
known as “Lula” – workers in the industrial&nbsp;
heartland around São Paulo went on strike.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.960
Lula understood well the indignities of the&nbsp;
working class. His parents were illiterate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.360
peasants who struggled to give their kids a&nbsp;
better life. He didn’t learn to read until he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.680
was 10. When he was 18, he crushed his pinky&nbsp;
in a workplace accident. By the time he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.080
seen by a doctor, four hours later, there&nbsp;
was no choice but to amputate the finger.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.520
Driven by these experiences, Lula and those&nbsp;
around him realized that workers needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:25.120
their own political party. So, in 1980, they&nbsp;
created the “Partido dos Trabalhadores” – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.480
Workers’ Party. It was the first party in&nbsp;
Brazil created for workers, by workers.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.000
The Workers’ Party grew quickly. And in 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.960
after three failed attempts,&nbsp;
Lula was elected president.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.440
In office, Lula recognized that&nbsp;
Brazil’s fundamental problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.480
was the grinding poverty and inequality that&nbsp;
previous governments had mostly ignored.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.240
So in 2003, Lula’s administration introduced&nbsp;
“Bolsa Família” – “the family stipend.”

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.560
Basically, the government would guarantee&nbsp;
an income to families in “extreme poverty,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.640
those earning less than 17 dollars per person per&nbsp;
month. And when poor families over the official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.160
poverty line were expecting a child or had a&nbsp;
child below 18, they would get extra income too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.400
As long as families get vaccinations&nbsp;
and keep their kids in school, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.200
can receive benefits indefinitely. There is no&nbsp;
mandatory drug testing, and no work requirement.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:23.120
With this guaranteed monthly income, poor families&nbsp;
can meet their basic needs and afford safer living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:28.400
conditions. The program doesn’t provide a lot by&nbsp;
American standards. Most families receive about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.440
38 dollars a month. But for the neediest&nbsp;
Brazilians, it has been transformational.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.440
Compared to what other countries were doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:40.160
the scale of Bolsa Família was incredible.&nbsp;
In the 1990s, U.S. President Bill Clinton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:45.520
had implemented “welfare reform.” He made it&nbsp;
more difficult to access government benefits.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.200
Promising to “end welfare as we know it,” he&nbsp;
instituted work requirements and drug tests.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.840
It became so complicated and humiliating&nbsp;
to secure government assistance that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.480
Americans who needed support received&nbsp;
no help at all. Extreme poverty soared.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.040
Brazil’s radical experiment, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:09.120
had the exact opposite effect. Bolsa Familia&nbsp;
quickly became an international success story.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:17.520
For one thing, it dramatically reduced poverty.&nbsp;
By 2015, the percentage of the population living

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.960
below the international poverty line had&nbsp;
dropped from 13 percent to just three.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:29.200
Bolsa Família helped lift over 40 million&nbsp;
people out of extreme poverty. In one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.880
the most unequal countries in the world, it has&nbsp;
cut wealth inequality by as much as 20 percent.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:41.120
Bolsa Família has also significantly reduced child&nbsp;
labor, and improved educational outcomes for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.920
poorest children. Studies suggest the program&nbsp;
has helped lower rates of domestic violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.160
postpartum depression, suicide,&nbsp;
and even tooth decay in children.&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.400
Bolsa Família isn’t just a cash transfer program.&nbsp;
It’s a successful attempt to wrestle with a legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:02.400
of social neglect. For many Brazilians, Bolsa&nbsp;
Família is the most tangible example of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.640
state acting on their behalf. It represents&nbsp;
the very promise of citizenship. It directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.360
attacks poverty in the present, enabling&nbsp;
families to invest in their children’s future.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.480
Lula left office in 2010 as one of&nbsp;
the most popular leaders on Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:24.640
and the most popular in Brazilian history. Bolsa&nbsp;
Família was a major reason for that. In fact, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.160
a far-right extremist won the presidency in 2018,&nbsp;
he sought ways to rebrand the program as his own.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.760
So while the United States sought to push people&nbsp;
out of poverty by forcing them off of welfare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.520
socialists in Brazil did the exact opposite:&nbsp;
they opened government support to more people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.680
In the U.S., poverty went&nbsp;
up. In Brazil, under Lula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:51.680
poverty shrunk, inequality fell, and the&nbsp;
economy grew to five times its size in 2002.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:59.520
Now, Brazil is still marked by serious inequality.&nbsp;
But the Workers’ Party showed that governments can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:06.560
act to dramatically reduce poverty. The first step&nbsp;
is recognizing that poor people don’t become less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.680
poor if their government calls them lazy, or makes&nbsp;
them take a drug test just to afford groceries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.280
Enable families to meet their material needs, and they’ll lead better lives.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.200
Lula remains popular in Brazil because&nbsp;
he acknowledged that everyone deserves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.720
a dignified life, regardless of their class&nbsp;
or income. And as we confront the rot that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.240
prosperous elites in this country&nbsp;
have allowed to fester for too long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.840
we might learn something from the&nbsp;
example of the Workers’ Party in Brazil.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.000
I’m Andre Pagliarini, professor of history&nbsp;
and Latin American studies at Dartmouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:47.840
for the Gravel Institute.

